...and no further output. Monitor's own 2,100,000ms (35min) timeout fired at that
point with zero further watchdog check-ins ever printed — diagnosed afterward
(attempt 2's own design notes) as an unbuffered-vs-fully-buffered `tee` bug: the
watchdog's log() function piped through `tee -a` to a non-tty consumer (Monitor),
which fully-buffered instead of line-buffered, so every check-in the loop actually
printed sat in a buffer and was lost when the process was hard-killed at timeout
rather than exiting normally and flushing. Whether the loop's checks ran normally
in the background the whole time (and were merely never seen) or genuinely never
executed cannot be distinguished after the fact — this is exactly why attempt 1
must be recorded as INCOMPLETE, not as evidence of anything about the gate itself.
